Antipsychotic-Induced Extrapyramidal Symptoms

1. Patient Details

Patient Initials / Identifier: L.M.

Age: 22 years

Gender: Male

Weight: 78 kg

2. Suspected Adverse Reaction

Description of Reaction: Acute dystonic reaction featuring involuntary neck muscle spasms (torticollis) and upward eye deviation (oculogyric crisis).

Date of Reaction Onset: Day 2 after dose escalation.

Severity: Moderate (caused severe distress and functional impairment).

3. Suspected Medication(s)

Primary Suspect: Haloperidol 5 mg orally, twice daily for acute psychosis.

Date Started: Day 1

Date Stopped: Day 3

Action Taken: Haloperidol discontinued; patient switched to an atypical antipsychotic (Olanzapine).

4. Concomitant Medications

Medication: None.

5. Outcome & Action Taken

Action Taken: Haloperidol stopped; intramuscular Diphenhydramine administered for immediate symptom reversal.

De-challenge Outcome: Dystonic spasms resolved within 45 minutes of anticholinergic administration.

Re-challenge: Not performed with high-potency typical antipsychotics.

Patient Outcome: Recovered completely without lasting neurological issues.
